==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|grandeur}}, from {{der|en|fro|grandur}}, from {{m|fro|grant}} ({{cog|fr|grand}}), from {{der|en|la|grandis|t=grown up, great}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɹæn.d͡ʒɚ/|/ˈɡɹæn.d͡ʒʊɚ/|/ˈɡɹæn.d(j)ʊɚ/|/ˈɡɹæn.d(j)ɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-grandeur.wav|Audio (US)}}
** {{homophones|en|grander|q1=one pronunciation}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of being [[grand]] or [[splendid]]; [[magnificence]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1829|author=Edgar Allan Poe|authorlink=Edgar Allan Poe|chapter={{w|Tamerlane (poem)|Tamerlane}}|title=Al Aaraaf, Tamerlane and Minor Poems|passage=I wrapp’d myself in '''grandeur''' then,<br>And donn’d a visionary crown ——}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 August 26|author=Tim Dunn|title=Great railway bores of our time!|journal=Rail|page=44|text=So much of what followed from the drawing boards of others will have been designed with the demands, effort and '''grandeur''' of Primrose Hill <small>[tunnel]</small> in the back of their minds.}}
# [[nobility|Nobility]] (state of being noble).
# {{lb|en|archaic|rare}} [[greatness|Greatness]]; [[largeness]]; [[tallness]]; [[loftiness]].

